In the summer of 2004, three scientists, Neil Shubin, Ted Daeschler and Farish Jenkins, were digging on Ellesmere Island in Arctic Canada. It's a frozen, treeless place where polar bears roam. They found a flat skull poking out of the rock.

It belonged to a creature they named Tiktaalik, which means "large freshwater fish" in Inuktitut, the language of local Inuit people. It grew to perhaps 2.5 m long, with scales and fins like a fish. But it had a flat head like a crocodile and a neck that let it turn its head. Its front fins had bones like a shoulder, elbow and wrist, strong enough for push-ups in shallow water.

{{story:fossils-form}}

Around 1811, Joseph found a strange long-snouted skull in the cliffs. Over the next year, Mary, then about 12, dug out the rest of the skeleton. It was an ichthyosaur, a dolphin-shaped [[reptile|reptile]] that swam in the seas about 200 million years ago. At the time, many people called it a crocodile or a "sea dragon".

In 1823, Mary found a nearly complete plesiosaur, with a tiny head, a very long neck and four flippers. One leading French scientist at first suspected it was a fake. After studying it, he changed his mind.

{{story:ocean-reptiles}}

If the solid Earth stopped instantly, your body would keep moving at that speed. So would buildings, trees and the [[atmosphere|atmosphere]]. Winds near the equator would be several times faster than the strongest tornado. Oceans would surge over coastlines as giant waves.

Scientists use this example to show why it can't happen. To stop Earth that fast would take an enormous force, more than anything in the [[solar-system|solar system]] could deliver.

{{story:day-night}}

The Moon's [[gravity|gravity]] pulls the oceans into two bulges, one facing the Moon and one on the far side. As Earth turns, coasts pass through them, giving high and low tide twice a day. The Sun does this too, but less than half as strongly.

With only the Sun, the sea would still rise and fall, just much less. The rock pools where crabs and starfish live would shrink. Some scientists think tides helped early sea creatures move onto land. Stranded for hours at low tide, they slowly got used to air, an idea explored in [[@moon-tides-life|how the Moon may have shaped life]].

{{story:moon-formed}}

For a long time, scientists said this should be impossible. A basic rule of physics, the conservation of angular momentum, says a spinning object with nothing to push on can't start spinning on its own. Yet cats seemed to do it.

In 1894 a French scientist, Étienne-Jules Marey, used a camera that took many pictures a second to film a falling cat. The photos, among the first ever to freeze such a quick movement, showed the twist clearly. Some members of the French Academy of Sciences still thought there must be a trick. It took until 1969 for two American engineers, Thomas Kane and M. P. Scher, to build a proper mathematical model.

{{story:ice-skating}}

Each nostril can smell separately. Dogs can tell which side a smell is stronger on, and turn towards it. That helps them follow a trail, swinging their heads from side to side.

Dogs can even smell time, in a way. A trail left an hour ago smells stronger than one from yesterday. By sniffing a few footsteps, a dog can work out which direction a person went. Bloodhounds, bred for tracking, have followed trails more than a day old. In the USA, evidence from trained bloodhounds has been allowed in some court cases.

{{story:dogs-domestic}}

Mud has very little air, and salt kills most plants. [[@mangroves|Mangroves]] have found ways around both. Many send up pencil-like roots that poke out of the mud like snorkels, letting them take in air. Some push extra salt out through their leaves. Others have arching stilt roots that hold them steady in the soft mud as the tides come and go.

The roots also trap mud and slow down water. That turns the forest into a shield. When strong [[cyclone|cyclones]] such as Aila in 2009 and Amphan in 2020 hit the coast, the mangroves softened the force of the storm surges for villages behind them.

{{story:mangroves}}

In 1854, cholera swept through Soho, a crowded part of [[@london|London]]. Many people believed diseases spread through bad-smelling air. A doctor named John Snow suspected the water. He marked every death on a map and found that most were clustered around one public water pump on Broad Street.

Snow convinced local officials to remove the pump's handle so nobody could use it. New cases fell away, though the outbreak was already slowing. Later it was found that a leaking cesspit sat right next to the well. Snow's map became one of the most famous in science, and it helped show that dirty water spreads disease.

{{story:toilets-sewers}}

कीर्तन में एक ऐसा खिंचाव होता है जिससे बचना मुश्किल है। यह आम तौर पर धीरे शुरू होता है, [[@harmonium|हारमोनियम]] पर एक सीधी-सादी धुन बजती है और ढोलक या तबले जैसा कोई ढोल [[rhythm|ताल]] सँभालता है। मंजीरे थाप पर खनकते हैं। मुखिया गाता है, मंडली जवाब देती है, और रफ़्तार बढ़ती जाती है।

1500 के दशक में बंगाल में चैतन्य महाप्रभु भीड़ के साथ कृष्ण के नाम गाते हुए गलियों में निकलते थे। इस शैली को संकीर्तन कहते हैं। संगीत का अध्ययन करने वाले वैज्ञानिकों ने पाया है कि मिलकर गाने से लोग एक-दूसरे के ज़्यादा करीब महसूस करते हैं, और शायद इसीलिए सामूहिक गायन लगभग हर संस्कृति में मिलता है।

{{story:harmonium}}

In March 1974, loggers arrived at the forest near Reni, a village in Chamoli district. Most of the men were away, called to collect government payments for land.

A woman named Gaura Devi, head of the village women's group, gathered about 27 women and girls. They walked into the forest and told the loggers that cutting the trees would mean cutting them first. The women stayed all night. The loggers gave up and left.

{{story:landslides}}

In 1889, Germany's chancellor Otto von Bismarck set up one of the first national old-age pension systems. Workers and employers paid in, and the state added money. The payments began at 70, at a time when few labourers lived that long.

Other countries followed. In the USA, the Great Depression of the 1930s left millions of older people with no savings and no work. In 1935 President Franklin D. Roosevelt signed the Social Security Act. The first monthly cheque went to Ida May Fuller of Vermont in January 1940, for $22.54. She had paid in only a few years of contributions, but she lived to 100.

{{story:great-depression}}

In 1936, an American magazine called the *Literary Digest* mailed out about 10 million ballots asking who would win the presidential election. Around 2.4 million came back, and the magazine predicted a big win for Alf Landon.

Franklin D. Roosevelt won by a landslide. The magazine had found its names in phone books and car registration lists, and in the 1930s many poorer Americans had neither a phone nor a car. A young pollster called George Gallup asked far fewer people, but chose them more carefully, and got the winner right.

{{story:statistics-lies}}

India's biggest parade happens every 26 January. It marks the day in 1950 when the [[@constitution|Constitution]] came into force and India became a [[republic|republic]]. The first parade, in 1950, was held in a stadium, and for a few years the venue changed. Since 1955, the parade has moved along the grand avenue that runs from the President's house towards India Gate, now called Kartavya Path.

Soldiers, sailors and air force crews march in step. Camels of the Border Security Force swing by in decorated saddles. States send tableaux, giant moving displays on trucks that show their festivals, crafts and history. A foreign leader is usually invited as chief guest. Then comes the flypast, with aircraft roaring low overhead in formation.

{{story:constitution}}

Most curling free kicks spin around eight to ten times a second and leave the boot faster than 100 km/h. That's quicker than a car on an expressway.

The curve is gentle at first, because the ball is moving fast. As air resistance slows it, the spin stays nearly the same, so the bend gets stronger in the last few metres. That late swerve is what beats goalkeepers. It's also why a well-hit corner seems to dive towards the goal at the end.

{{story:cricket-swing}}

तुम्हारा शरीर खुद ऐसे [[enzyme|एंज़ाइम]] बनाता है जो स्टार्च, प्रोटीन और वसा को तोड़ते हैं। यह काम ज़्यादातर छोटी आँत में होता है। पर इंसान ज़्यादातर रेशा नहीं पचा पाते। वह लगभग जस का तस कोलन तक पहुँच जाता है।

वहाँ बैक्टीरिया उसे खाते हैं। उसे तोड़ते हुए वे गैसें छोड़ते हैं (इसीलिए राजमा और छोले खाकर [[@gross-farts|पेट में गैस बनती है]]) और कुछ छोटे अणु भी, जिन्हें शॉर्ट-चेन फ़ैटी एसिड कहते हैं। कोलन की कोशिकाएँ इन्हें खाने की तरह इस्तेमाल करती हैं। डॉक्टर और खान-पान के जानकार इसीलिए भी खूब फल, सब्ज़ियाँ, दालें और साबुत अनाज खाने की सलाह देते हैं।

{{story:fermentation}}

बहुत-से जानवर वे चीज़ें भाँप लेते हैं जो हम नहीं भाँप सकते। शार्क और रे मछलियों की थूथन पर जेली से भरे बारीक छेद होते हैं। ये दूसरे जानवरों की मांसपेशियों से निकलने वाले हल्के बिजली के संकेत पकड़ लेते हैं। इसी से [[@sharks-old|शार्क]] रेत में दबी मछली भी ढूँढ लेती है। प्लैटिपस भी कुछ ऐसा ही करता है।

पिट वाइपर जैसे कुछ साँप अँधेरे में गरम खून वाले शिकार की गरमी भाँप लेते हैं। बहुत-सी चिड़ियाँ, कछुए और दूसरे जानवर [[@earth-magnet|धरती के चुंबकीय क्षेत्र]] को महसूस कर लेते हैं और लंबे [[@migration|प्रवास]] में इसी से रास्ता ढूँढते हैं। चमगादड़ और डॉल्फ़िन गूँज के सहारे आवाज़ से "देखते" हैं।

{{story:echolocation}}

ज़्यादातर [[mammal|स्तनधारियों]] को ज़्यादा पसीना नहीं आता। कुत्ते जीभ बाहर निकालकर हाँफते हैं। बिल्लियाँ अपने बाल चाटती हैं। हाथी अपने बड़े-बड़े कान फड़फड़ाते हैं। इंसान, हालाँकि, पूरे शरीर से पसीना बहा सकते हैं और गरमी में घंटों चलते रह सकते हैं।

कुछ वैज्ञानिक मानते हैं कि इससे शुरुआती इंसानों को शिकार में मदद मिली। "पीछा करके थकाने" वाले शिकार में लोग दोपहर की गरमी में हिरन जैसे जानवरों का पीछा करते थे। जानवर तेज़ दौड़ते थे, पर उन्हें हाँफने और ठंडा होने के लिए रुकना पड़ता था। इंसान पसीना बहाते हुए चलते रहते, जब तक जानवर इतना गरम न हो जाए कि दौड़ ही न पाए। दक्षिणी अफ़्रीका के कुछ शिकारी हाल के समय तक यह तरीका इस्तेमाल करते थे। शायद इसी वजह से लोग [[@running-marathon|मैराथन]] दौड़ भी पाते हैं।

{{story:running-marathon}}

फेफड़ों की टीबी के सबसे आम लक्षण हैं दो-तीन हफ़्ते से ज़्यादा चलने वाली खाँसी, बुख़ार, रात में पसीना, वज़न घटना और बहुत थकान। जिसे भी ऐसी खाँसी हो, उसे डॉक्टर को दिखाना चाहिए। भारत में सरकारी स्वास्थ्य केंद्रों पर टीबी की जाँच और दवाएँ मुफ़्त हैं।

1943 में अमेरिका के वैज्ञानिकों ने स्ट्रेप्टोमाइसिन खोजी, जो टीबी पर काम करने वाली पहली एंटीबायोटिक थी। आज मरीज़ कई दवाओं का मेल लेते हैं, क्योंकि टीबी के कीटाणु किसी एक दवा से बचना सीख सकते हैं। पूरा कोर्स ख़त्म करना बेहद ज़रूरी है। बीच में दवा छोड़ देना एक बड़ी वजह है कि दवाओं पर असर न होने वाली टीबी एक गंभीर मुसीबत बन गई है।

{{story:microscopes}}

India was the biggest colony to break free. After a long [[@freedom-struggle|freedom struggle]], British India became independent in August 1947 and was divided into India and Pakistan, a [[@partition|partition]] that cost many lives. Burma (now Myanmar) and Ceylon (now [[@sri-lanka|Sri Lanka]]) followed in 1948.

India's independence inspired movements across Asia and Africa. Indonesia declared independence from the Netherlands in 1945 and won it after four years of fighting. [[@vietnam|Vietnam]]'s struggle against France, and later against the United States, lasted decades.

{{story:flags-world}}

Rodgers had a secret helper below him. A special train followed his route, carrying his wife Mabel, his mechanics and a whole hangar's worth of spare parts. Pilots of the time often steered by following railway lines, so the [[@transcontinental-railroad|railroad across America]] doubled as his map.

He needed that train. He crashed on take-off and on landing, again and again. He was hurt more than once and at one point carried his crutches strapped to the plane. By the time he reached California, nearly every part had been replaced. Only the vertical rudder and a drip pan from the original plane are said to have made it the whole way.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

Samuel Pepys was a government official in [[@london|London]] in the 1660s. For nearly ten years he kept a diary, in a shorthand that few people could read. He wrote about plays, food, arguments with his wife and gossip at work.

He also wrote about huge events. In 1665 he described the plague, and in 1666 the Great Fire of London. As flames spread, he buried his wine and a Parmesan cheese in his garden to save them. His diary is now one of the best sources historians have on life in London at that time.

{{story:lewis-clark}}

Egyptians watched dung beetles roll balls of dung across the sand and bury them. Later, new beetles crawled out, as if born from nothing. They pictured a giant scarab god, Khepri, rolling the Sun across the sky the same way.

Scarab charms became some of the most common lucky objects in Egypt, worn by the living and placed with the dead.

{{story:power-dung-beetle}}

This kind of book uses a [[frame-story|frame story]], an outer tale that holds many smaller ones inside, like boxes in a box. The [[@arabian-nights|Arabian Nights]] work the same way, with Scheherazade telling a new story each night. The [[@panchatantra-tales|Panchatantra]] does it too, with a teacher using animal stories to educate three princes.

The Betal stories appear in the Kathasaritsagara, a huge collection of tales written down in Sanskrit by a poet named Somadeva in Kashmir in the 11th century. They were later retold in many languages. A Hindi version called Baital Pachisi became popular in the 1800s, and the explorer Richard Burton put out an English version in 1870.

{{story:puzzles-logic}}

When Spanish explorers reached the Caribbean in the late 1400s, they saw the Taíno people cooking and drying food on frames of green sticks raised over a fire. The Spanish wrote the word as *barbacoa*. Most historians think this is the root of "barbecue", though nobody can be completely sure.

Over the next centuries, cooking whole animals over pits of coals became common in the American colonies. [[@george-washington|George Washington]] wrote in his diary about going "to a barbicue".

{{story:fire-burns}}

Mughal emperors loved mangoes. [[@akbar|Akbar]] is said to have planted an orchard of about one lakh trees near Darbhanga in Bihar, and his court kept careful notes on the best kinds. Mughal writers described mangoes with great admiration. Nawabs, rajas and wealthy landlords competed to own rare varieties, and gardeners became skilled at grafting.

Many famous mangoes carry stories in their names. The Dasheri is named after a village near Lucknow where, local people say, the first tree still stands, about 200 years old. The Langra, from Varanasi, means "lame", and is said to be named after a farmer who walked with a limp.

{{story:genes-family}}

Most crops die if their roots sit in water, because roots need oxygen. Rice has a trick: spongy air channels run down its stems, like straws carrying air to the roots.

So farmers flood their paddies, often with [[@monsoon|monsoon]] rain. Weeds drown, the rice does not. Planting by hand in knee-deep mud is hard work, and in many [[@villages-india|Indian villages]] it is still done in groups, with songs to keep the [[rhythm|rhythm]].

{{story:monsoon}}

Raw dried corn is hard and difficult to digest, and grinding it does not fix that. Thousands of years ago, people in Mexico discovered that cooking corn in water with lime makes a big difference. This lime is calcium hydroxide, a white mineral powder made from limestone or shells.

The process is called [[nixtamalisation|nixtamalisation]]. It loosens the corn's tough skin, makes the dough stick together, adds calcium and frees niacin, one of the B [[@vitamins|vitamins]]. When corn spread to Europe and the American South without this trick, many poor people who ate mostly corn fell ill with a disease called pellagra, caused by a lack of niacin.

